    private void Start()
    {
        myCaptions.Add("Story 1: Tales from the Forest - The Story of the Doe and the She-Wolf");
        myCaptions.Add("Story 2: A Tale of Love Beyond Borders");
        myCaptions.Add("Story 3: The Miner’s Tale");
        myCaptions.Add("Story 4: The Tale of the Dark Queen");
        myCaptions.Add("Story 5: The Tale of the Young Duke");
        myCaptions.Add("Story 6: The Tale of the Last Elf and the Last Earth Sprite");
        myCaptions.Add("Story 7: The Tale of the Silent Keepers");
        myCaptions.Add("Story 8: The Tale of the Man Who Watched Over the Light");
        myCaptions.Add("Story 9: The Tale of the Last Oath of Kareth");
        myCaptions.Add("Story 10: A Tale of Two Sisters");
        myCaptions.Add("Story 11: Tales from the Forest - The Saga of the Old Green");
        myCaptions.Add("Story 12: A Tale of Friendship Beyond Time");
        myCaptions.Add("Story 13: Dashuri - Tales from the Magic Forest Part 1");
        myCaptions.Add("Story 14: Dashuri - Tales from the Magic Forest Part 2");
        myCaptions.Add("Story 15: Dashuri - Tales from the Magic Forest Part 3");
        myCaptions.Add("Story 16: Dashuri - Tales from the Magic Forest Part 4");

        myTexts.Add("Long before roads carved their way through the land, and before humankind learned to give every tree a name, there was a Forest that breathed like one living being. Its roots reached deep into the earth, its crowns whispered the secrets of the World, and its creatures lived by laws older—and more precious—than any crown or coin ever shaped by human hands.In this Forest lived a doe, slender and grey-brown, swift as fear and gentle as the morning mist. She had beenborn in a harsh spring, when frost lingered too long and many fawns never found their footing. She survived only because of another animal. That animal was a she-wolf.It happened near the bend of the river, where the Forest floor dipped low and reeds grew thick. The fawn waslittle more than a trembling bundle of legs and breath, caught in the rising waters after the thaw had burst the riverbanks.Its mother had panicked, circled, called—and then fled when the scent of predators reached her.The wolf had not come to hunt, but to drink.She was young then, scarcely grown. Her own pack had only recently been scattered by hunger and conflict. She had learned to survive alone, teeth sharp, ribs visible, eyes ever searching. When she heard the cry—thinand desperate—something within her made her pause.\r\nStories_of_the_Wind_Collectibles.odt\r\nA young fawn was drowning.The wolf waded into the water. Cold bit into her legs and sOaked her fur, but she pressed forward and took the fawn by the scruff with a gentleness she had never shown prey before. She dragged the small body to the shore and stood over it, breathing heavily, torn between instinct and something she had no name for.The fawn lived.The doe’s mother returned at dusk, marked by terror, her eyes wild, and found her child alive. She saw the wolf standing nearby. The mother was ready to flee—or to fight in desperation. For a long moment, the Forest held its breath.Then the mother lowered her head—not in submission, but in recognition. The wolf stepped back and vanished among the trees, carrying with her a memory she would never understand, yet never forget.The Wind rustled through the branches as the wolf slipped into the forest’s shadows.Years passed.The fawn grew into a doe, strong and sure-footed. She learned every hidden path, every safe hollow, every warning sign of the seasons. She bore fawns of her own and taught them when to run, when to stand still, andwhen to trust the silence of the forest.In that time, the wolf became a leader.She gathered strays around her—hungry loners, wounded females, pups who had lost their mothers. She taught them to hunt without waste, to kill without cruelty, and to honor the Forest by keeping its balance. Under her guidance, the pack endured winters that should have broken them.Neither clearly remembered the other. Time softens even the sharpest moments. And yet something remained—a bond. An unspoken thread, woven from breath and water and mercy.And the Wind carried it patiently.Then came a winter harsher than any before. Snow fell early and heavy, burying food, silencing rivers, and driving hunger deep into the bones. The wolves grew desperate. Even under their leader’s restraint, the pack crept closer to the deer paths than ever before.That was when the hunters came.Men with iron traps, fire-scented sticks, and voices too loud for the forest. They came for the wolves—but cared little what else they killed.One morning, the doe—older now, her steps slower, yet made wise by the years—led her fawns across a clearing she had crossed a thousand times before.The trap lay hidden beneath the snow.Metal snapped shut. Bone broke. Her cry tore through the forest.She struggled, kicked, bled—but iron yields neither to fear nor to the desperate strength of a weakend creature. As darkness fell, wolves howled in the distance, and the doe knew she would not see another dawn.Her fawns hid and watched, helpless.A bitter Wind blew.The wolf reached the clearing first.\r\nStories_of_the_Wind_Collectibles.odt\r\nShe smelled blood, iron—and something else. Something faintly familiar, beyond naming. She saw the doe caught fast, weakened to a point where she was barely holding on to life, her eyes still proud, yet already dimming. The pack gathered behind her, tense, hungry, uncertain.This was easy prey. This was survival. This was balance.And yet.The wolf stepped forward alone. She circled the doe as memories stirred within her—like half-forgotten dreams of cold water and a life once spared. A memory of how a given life had once brought her comfort in the face of loss and loneliness. She heard the faint whimpering of the hidden fawns and felt something in her chest begin to break open.She lunged at the trap.Metal bit into her teeth, cut into her gums, but she tore at it with a fury beyond anything she had ever shown. The pack joined her—not to kill, but to free.Together they bent iron, broke stakes, and tore apart the chains.The doe collapsed, freed, yet still dying. The voices of the hunters echoed nearby.The wolf made a choice.She placed herself between the doe and the approaching men and howled—not a threat, but a command. Her pack scattered, drawing the hunters away, leading them deep into the forest, where paths twisted and the snow swallowed every sound.They returned at dawn, wounded—but alive.The doe no longer lived. But her fawns did.Their mother had died in peace, knowing her children were unharmed. They stood at the edge of the clearing,trembling, and watched as the wolves came back. The wolf met their gaze but did not approach. She lowered her head once—then turned away.The Forest fell silent again.Years later, the wolf had grown very old.Her muzzle had turned grey, her steps slow. One spring, she lay down beneath an ancient Oak and did not rise again. Her pack mourned her first with loud howls, and then in deep silence, as wolves do, before moving on.That same spring, a young doe led her herd safely past a familiar bend in the river. She paused, her ears twitching, and lifted her nose to the air—as though sensing something long gone.The Wind moved through the trees, gentle and knowing.Perhaps it was chance that the descendants of the doe always came to drink at that place.Perhaps it was instinct.Or perhaps the Forest remembers kindness better than cruelty.Only the Wind knows the truth.");
        myTexts.Add("There was a time when the World was already divided, though not yet by walls or war. It was divided in thought—by fear, by forgetting, and by the slow fading of belief in, and memory of, magic.On one side of the boundary lay the lands of humankind, where fields were measured, names of importance were written down, and everything had its place and purpose. On the other side remained what was left of theold World: forests where paths shifted when no one watched, rivers that remembered songs, and beings whose existence depended not on laws, but on stories.Between these lands stretched a wide meadow. No one claimed it. No one guarded it. And so it remained free.There, they met.The boy arrived for the first time. He was not meant to wander so far. His village lay a day’s journey away, and his parents believed that boundaries kept children safe. But the boy had always followed the Wind more readily than rules. He liked the way it tugged at his hair, carried scents from distant places, and whispered of unseen things.One afternoon, chasing a paper bird he had folded himself, he crossed into the meadow. And there, sitting in the tall grass, was a child he had never seen before.She looked almost like him—and yet not at all. Her ears were slightly pointed. Her eyes held the reflection ofthe sky a moment longer than they should. When she smiled, the flowers leaned toward her, as if listening.They stared at one another in silence. Then they laughed. They became friends, without ever naming it.\r\nStories_of_the_Wind_Collectibles.odt\r\nThey met again the next day. And the next. Always in the meadow, always in that place where neither World felt entirely real. They shared bread and berries, stories and questions. They shaped little figurines from clay and thread, and pretended they were the guardians of the meadow.The boy spoke of his village, of school and work, and of how the adults argued endlessly over things that never seemed to matter. The girl spoke of Forest gatherings, of spirits dancing at dusk, and of songs that could heal both broken bark and broken hearts.They never asked, or even questioned why they were different. To children, difference is something to be wondered at, never feared.But boundaries do not remain soft forever.The boy stopped coming. Days passed. Then weeks. The meadow grew restless. The Wind circled, searching.When the boy finally returned, he had changed. He was older. Quieter. His laughter hesitated, as though it had learned caution. He told her his family was leaving, that soldiers now guarded the roads, and that his parents said it was dangerous to wander. He did not know why only he could find the path to the meadow. No one had ever followed him there, though he had often thought about sharing that wondrous place. But he never did.“They say your kind isn’t real,” he said, staring into the grass.“They say believing in you is childish.”The girl did not cry. But the flowers around her closed their petals.“They say your people have forgotten us,” she answered softly. “They say the door binding our Lands is closing.”That day, they held each other’s hands for a long time, and neither knew how to say farewell. So they stayed where they were, as though they sensed that letting go would mean parting forever.A storm rose that night, sudden and fierce. When morning came, the meadow was scarred, the paths erased. The boundary hardened. And the children were drawn back into their own Worlds, like leaves carried into separate currents.Years passed.The boy became a man. He learned a trade, learned restraint, learned to hide wonder behind usefulness. He married, raised children, told them stories—but never this one. Somewhere along the way, he convinced himself the meadow had been a dream.The girl grew as well—but differently. Time bends strangely for those bound to magic. She watched the forests become thin, the spirits fade, the songs fall silent. She waited at the meadow long after it had ceased to feel like a meeting place, and had instead begun to resemble a grave.And yet she never forgot his face.Even the Wind missed the presence of the last human who had once been able to listen. It remembered for them both. One autumn, when the World felt thinner than usual, the man crossed the meadow once more. He did not know why—or how it was possible. His feet simply carried him there. Perhaps it was grief—his wife had died during past winter. Perhaps it was the weariness of a life lived carefully, cautiously, correctly.Or perhaps it was love—unfinished, and unforgotten.\r\nStories_of_the_Wind_Collectibles.odt\r\nShe was there. Older, yes—but unmistakable.They knew each other at once—not by sight, but by the way the Wind rushed between them, breathless and eager. This time, they spoke as adults. Of loss. Of regret. Of the quiet pain of having lived only half the lives their hearts had once imagined.“I thought you weren’t real,” he confessed. “I thought you were a story I had told myself.”She smiled sadly. “I was real,” she said. “But stories need belief to survive.”They kissed—once, gently, as though they feared the World might break if they dared more.And then the Wind grew still.The decision was never spoken aloud. He could not stay. His body belonged to a World without magic. His children needed him. His time was measured, finite. She could not leave. The Forest needed witnesses. The old songs had to be preserved. If she crossed fully into his World, they would fade.So they loved each other from across the border of their Worlds.They met whenever the days allowed—sometimes weeks apart, sometimes years. And yet each time, it felt asthough only a moment had passed since they were last together. They aged at different paces. Their hands changed, their voices deepened and softened. But their love remained strangely untouched, held in the space between Worlds.When the man had grown old, he came one last time. He lay in the meadow grass, his breath shallow, his eyes kind.“Do you think,” he asked, “that this love mattered?”She knelt beside him and pressed her forehead to his. “It mattered,” she said. “It changed me. And it changedyou. That is enough.”When he took his final breath, the Wind rose—not in grief, but in reverence—and carried him gently into theforest.Long after both Worlds had forgotten their names, people spoke of a meadow where flowers bloomed even in winter, and where the air always felt warm, as if filled with memories of friendship and love.Some said it was a place of magic. Some said it was only a beautiful story. But lovers who crossed boundaries—of land, of belief, of fear—sometimes felt the Wind pause around them.As if remembering. As if smiling.And even across the greatest distances—that crossing of boundaries, once dared by two lovers, left behind a quiet gift: the sense that somewhere, their love had a place.Even if they never knew why, it gave them courage.The truth is known only to the Wind.");
        myTexts.Add("The village lay in a narrow valley, pressed between the mountains like a secret the World had chosen to keep. Its people were known neither for wealth nor for power, but for their endurance. Stone was their companion, their enemy, and their livelihood.Deep beneath the village ran the old tunnels.They had been carved long before things were given names—dug first by hands driven by need, later by hands that obeyed command. Layer upon layer, passage upon passage; some supported by timber and care, others held together only by habit and hope.And far below, deeper than most villagers ever dared to imagine, the miners worked.Or, as some called them: the Bound.When the earth groaned for the first time, it sounded like a distant storm. Dust fell from the beams. Lamps trembled. The miners froze and listened. They knew the language of stone better than anyone. This was no small warning. This was the mountain shifting its weight.Then the World broke.\r\nStories_of_the_Wind_Collectibles.odt\r\nWalls burst. Supports screamed. A thunder rolled through the tunnels as the main entrance collapsed in on itself, sealing the underworld away from the sky above. Darkness swallowed every sound. Men were thrown to the ground, buried beneath debris, crushed by falling rock.When the tremor ceased, silence followed—heavy and final.Those who still breathed counted one another by voice. They were fewer than before.Above ground, the village cried out and prayed. Dust rose from the earth like a funeral veil. The entrance to the mine was gone, buried beneath tons of stone. The people above dug until their hands bled. They listened for voices that never answered.At nightfall, the elders spoke the words no one wished to hear. “There is no air left.” “There is no way through.” “They are lost.”Candles were lit. Names were whispered. The village mourned. And the Wind carried their sorrow away.Below, the miners lived.For now.They found pockets of air—narrow and fragile—breathing through cracks that whispered faintly of the World above. They rationed their lamps, their food, their strength.And slowly, they understood the truth.The collapse had not only trapped them. It had split the mountain above the village. If the pressure shifted again—if the cavities gave way upward—houses, roads, and lives would be swallowed whole. The tunnels held the mountain in place. And if they, the trapped below, broke their way out......the village would die.Some still wanted to dig for escape. Not from selfishness—but from fear, from love for their families, from the simple human refusal to lie down and vanish without resistance.But others saw the stone differently.They were the oldest among them. The most experienced. The quiet ones. The ones who had last seen and felt the light of the sun before many of the others were born. Those who had learned to listen to the mountaininstead of fighting it.“If we open a path,” one of them said in a hoarse voice, “we will drag the sky down with us.”Silence followed.Then another spoke—a man whose wrists still bore the marks of iron. “We were never meant to live long lives,” he said. “But perhaps we can grant it to others.”Only then did they remember the truth—unspoken, yet understood by all.They were not free men.Most of the miners were slaves. Taken from distant lands, bought in chains or born into them. Sent beneath the earth. For stone does not care who bleeds upon it. But humans do. Overseers and guards had worked beside them that day—fewer in number, but armed, and accustomed to obedience.Now, for the first time, rank had lost all meaning.For the first time, there was a choice.\r\nStories_of_the_Wind_Collectibles.odt\r\nThe guards tried to command them. “Dig!” they shouted. “Clear the entrance. That is an order!”But the slaves did not move.Instead, they looked at one another—into faces marked by years of labor, at hands ruined by work that had never been intended to benefit them in any way, at lives spent being told their worth was less than the air they breathed.And in the darkness, they felt that air slipping away.One slave stepped forward. Then another.They did not attack. They did not flee. They held the guards in place—not with violence, but with resolve.“You will listen now,” they said. “Or we will all die as nothing.”At first, the guards resisted. They raised their weapons.Then they stopped.For courage—true courage—weighs more than any sword or armor.Together, they formed a plan.They would not dig outward. They would not clear the collapsed tunnels. Instead, they would reinforce them.Stones were moved. Gaps were filled. Weak points were braced with the last of the timber. They worked not outward, but inward—sacrificing space, sealing themselves layer by layer, to keep the mountain from collapsing above.With every wall they closed, the air grew thinner.With every support they set, the village above grew safer.Somewhere above, children slept peacefully through the night.In the final chamber, they could no longer see. They worked by touch, by memory, by trust. Through a narrow crack in the rock, they thought they could smell grass. Fresh air. Morning.They closed their eyes and imagined the World above.Someone laughed softly. Someone wept.No one tried to reach the crack. They did not wish to break the image.They sat together—slaves and guards, equal at last.“I am sorry,” one of the overseers whispered, his voice breaking. “I did not have your courage.”Hands that had once worn chains were now free. They rested gently upon his shoulders.The Wind slipped through the stone, impossibly soft, and brushed across their faces as their breaths grew shallow.In that final moment, they belonged to no one.They were free.Above the earth, the mountain held.The village endured.\r\nStories_of_the_Wind_Collectibles.odt\r\nYears later, change began—slowly, uncertainly, almost imperceptibly. Taskmasters grew kinder. Chains disappeared. People spoke of a tragedy that had buried miners and guards alike—a shared fate, a warning of the mountain’s cruelty.History books would much later claim to know the moment when hearts began to change. When people learned compassion. The moment when equality had risen from this historical moment of sorrow.But they were wrong.The truth lay deeper—buried beneath stone, resolve, and sacrifice.And no history book would ever tell it.Only the Wind knows the truth.");
        myTexts.Add("There was once a Queen whose beauty shone so brightly that she seemed like a beacon in a dark World. Butbeauty is often only a mask—a thin veil stretched over an abyss. The Wind that wandered through the cold corridors of her castle had long known, before any human did, that her heart was made of shards—sharp-edged, broken, and beyond repair.The story began with a king whose heart had sunk into deep sorrow after the death of his beloved wife. In hisloneliness, he sought comfort and found a woman whose origin was as mysterious as her nature—and her appearance. He did not ask from which distant lands she came, nor what scars her soul bore. He saw only hergrace and believed that in saving her, he might find his own redemption. He took her as his wife and crowned her Queen, hoping that love would drive out the darkness within her. For her skin was white as ice—but her heart seemed black.But he was wrong.The Queen did not carry her hatred as a whim, but as an inheritance. Her own family had done things to her so unspeakable that she had lost all belief in goodness. To her, the World was a place of watchers—people who had stood by as she broke, never once intervening. Her hatred was a slow poison, born of deep suffering. She wished to punish the World for continuing seemindly untouched, while the one being beaten down, the one who suffered had already died within.The king, a gentle man, could not withstand the cold within her. He withered away, consumed by the sorrow of a marriage consisting only of silence and frost, until he died. He left behind only a son—a prince whose heart was as gentle as his father’s.\r\nStories_of_the_Wind_Collectibles.odt\r\nAfter the king’s death, the grief of the people soon turned to fear. The dark Queen ruled with an iron hand. But her greatest cruelty she reserved for the boy. He reminded her of everything she despised: innocence, gentleness, and the hope that love might be enough to endure. Each day she found new ways to torment him. It was not only physical blows—it was the breaking of his spirit, the constant diminishing of his very existence.The prince endured the punishments without resistance. He did not see her as an enemy, but as a wounded creature that knew only how to bite. Within his heart burned a light her hatred could not extinguish. He watched his land suffer under her rule, as taxes rose and laughter vanished from the streets. The Queen reveled in the suffering of others; it was the only language she still understood.Then came a winter colder and more merciless than any before. The harvests had been poor, and the farmers’stores would scarcely last until the next turning of the moon. Yet instead of opening the granaries, the Queen planned to keep what remained for herself and send lavish gifts to her allies in distant lands, strengthening her power. She would let her people starve to raise her own standing.“Families are vermin,” she would say. “They breed lies and weakness. They must be destroyed to make way for something new.”The castle stood upon high cliffs, surrounded by massive walls and soldiers who carried out every command without hesitation. She felt safe in her ivory tower, high above the misery of the “simple peasants,” whom she scornfully called rats.On that fateful evening, she stepped onto the great balcony that overlooked the abyss. The Wind lashed against her, but she welcomed the cold.Then the prince stepped out from the shadows.For the first time, he did not retreat. He confronted her and begged her to change her decision—to give breadto the people instead of bringing death upon the land.The Queen only laughed. It was a hollow, ugly sound. She mocked him, called him weak like his father, and struck him again and again. She ridiculed his tears, his concern for the people.And in that moment, something within the prince changed.It was not hatred that moved him, but the realization that justice can sometimes take a terrible form.He pushed her away—a single, powerful motion.The Queen staggered. Her heels found no grip on the frozen stone, and she fell over the railing into the depths.And in that moment, as the ground vanished beneath her feet, the impossible occurred.As she fell, the cold Wind pressing against her face, something within her soul began to change. In the weightlessness of death, the layers of pain and cruelty fell away. The hatred she had worn like armor shattered against the air.Tears she had not shed since childhood ran down her cheeks and were carried away by the Wind. In her final moments, she saw not the enemies she had wished to punish, but the small moments of beauty she had denied herself. Regret flooded her like a warm tide.She saw the prince’s face in her mind—and with her last breath, she whispered into the roaring Wind words no one would have thought possible:“I forgive you... and I am sorry.”\r\nStories_of_the_Wind_Collectibles.odt\r\nFor the first time in her life, she felt no hatred.Only peace.When her body was found at the foot of the cliffs, everyone believed it had been a tragic accident. The princebore the marks of her blows, his wounds still fresh, and no one would ever have believed he had raised his hand against the Queen.He was crowned king, and under his rule, the land flourished once more.Later, the history books would claim that the wicked Queen had cursed her people until her final breath. They told of how she died with a scream of rage, and how her black heart had never known love. The people hated her memory, and her name became a synonym for cruelty.No one knew that she had died a different woman than she had lived.No one heard her forgiveness. No one saw her tears of regret. The World kept its image of the “evil Queen,” for people need monsters to feel righteous.No one would ever know what truly happened during the dark Queen’s fall.Only the Wind knows the truth.");

        myTexts.Add("There was once a ducal family that ruled over a small part of the land. They were wealthy, and each member of the family possessed their own residence and a retinue of servants; they lived better than most people would ever dare to dream. But the old Duke died and left the family’s throne to his son. As the boy had not yet come of age, he was to be raised and protected by his uncle until he was old enough to take his rightful place.The boy was cheerful and kind, one who brought a smile to everyone around him. And every smile was sincere—except that of his uncle. For he secretly desired the throne for himself and dreamed of one day seeing his own sons rule the land. Yet he was such a cruel man that his wife had left him before she could ever bear him children.The young Duke loved to explore the land and to befriend both nobles and farmers alike. His uncle allowed it, hoping that something might befall the boy and that he would die. But no one wished harm upon the child.On the contrary, everyone looked after him, and watched out for the boys well-being.One fateful day, the boy met the son of a farmer who lived somewhat apart from the villages he usually visited. The boy might have been his twin. When they set eyes on each other, it was like gazing into a mirror.They decided to keep this a secret and to be brothers to one another.At first, the young Duke never entered the farmer boy’s village, so as not to be seen by its people. But soon curiosity overcame him. So they decided to trade places for a week. They prepared everything. Having told each other nearly everything about their lives, there were few secrets left. Only the essentials remained to be taught.\r\nStories_of_the_Wind_Collectibles.odt\r\nThe young Duke taught his friend to read and write, the ways of etiquette, and all the things a Duke must know. And the farmer boy taught the young Duke how to plow the fields, read the weather, and tend to livestock.Then they exchanged places.Both were filled with excitement. For the farmer boy, a dream had come true; for the first time in his life, he did not have to work his body until it failed him. And yet he could not help himself—he swept the stables, and helped clear the table after supper. The servants only smiled and did not question it—had the young Duke not always been kind of heart?Meanwhile, the true young Duke explored the village, worked beneath the burning sun and the pouring rain, and within only a few days loved the farmer’s family and their home as though it were his own.Never in his life had he been happier.On the day they were to meet again, the young Duke saw his uncle riding out with his friend, the farmer boy, toward the marshes for a hunt. He followed them in secret, intending to return unnoticed and exchange places once more.But what he saw would haunt him for the rest of his life.The uncle looked around, and when he believed himself alone, he pushed the farmer boy into a deep part of the marsh.As the child cried out and reached out his hand in pleading, the uncle pressed him deeper into the swamp with his boot.The true young Duke cried out—but it was too late, and he was too far away to be heard. The Wind shook the branches loudly, and did not carry the sound of his voice over to the scene of this unspeakable crime.With a broken heart, the boy turned away.He thought of his friend’s family—and of how his own uncle had been capable of killing what he believed tobe his own flesh and blood. And the young Duke decided that his friend’s family should not lose their son, just as his own family had now lost the young Duke.So he continued to live the life of the farmer boy and did not see the members of his real family again for many years.The news of the death of a young noble and the mourning of the ducal family spread throughout the land. But after a time, it was forgotten, and life in the small village went on.Until one day, many years later, a voice within his heart called the once-young Duke—now a grown man—back to the marshes.He stood there in silence, as though waiting, remembering his friend.Each year, whenever he could, he brought flowers to the place in the marsh where his friend had died, and hepromised him that he would not forget. He made that promise each time he plowed the fields, each time he fed the animals, and each time he felt the loving embrace of his friend’s mother.In those moments, he let a part of the farmer boy—who had suffered such a cruel betrayal—live on within him, while he carried the joyful memories of their shared childhood in his heart.The uncle had stayed away from that place since that dark day many years ago. He now sat upon the throne, as he had always desired.\r\nStories_of_the_Wind_Collectibles.odt\r\nBut the gnawing weight of his guilt had aged him quickly and worn his body down. Having taken not only a young life—but, as he believed, his own blood—and having betrayed the trust of his brother and his entire family without ever being able to confess it, he had become a frail and broken old man.For the first time since that terrible act, the uncle returned to the place of his crime.It was the very day of the year when the boy had sunk into the depths of the marsh.He did not know that there was another who had never forgotten that day—who returned to this place each year at the same time.The uncle stood, staring into the swamp. He wondered at the bed of flowers that bloomed there, while nowhere else nearby anything wanted to grow.Then the young Duke stepped out from the bushes.The uncle stared at him, eyes widening in shock. Then he howled as though he had seen a ghost, cried out forforgiveness, and in his fear and confusion ran deep into the marsh.He was never seen again.The young Duke returned to his farm. Later, he learned that an aunt of his had taken the throne and married to continue the family line.The uncle, however, died alone—unseen, in fear and torment.No one would ever know.The truth is only known to the Wind.");
        myTexts.Add("There came a time, as it always does in the great cycle of the World, when magic did not vanish all at once.But it grew thin.Its strength faded, like a breath that leaves less mist upon the glass each morning, until one day it no longer clouds it at all—until no one passing on the other side can see that someone still lives there.At first, humankind did not notice.Instead, they noticed progress. Stone replaced wood. Fire was tamed. Words were written down instead of only sung and spoken. The World grew louder, brighter, faster—and somehow emptier.And slowly, almost gently... people forgot.Deep within the Forest of Shpresa stood the Great Golden Oak. Once, its leaves had glowed like the sunrise, and its roots had carried magic through the World like blood through veins. Now its bark had darkened, its light reduced to a faint, weary pulse.And yet its heart still beat.For somewhere in the World, one child still believed.That belief—fragile, stubborn, and beautiful—was enough.And so it came to be that two last small magical beings still remained.\r\nStories_of_the_Wind_Collectibles.odt\r\nThe last Elf was no larger than a child’s forearm. She shimmered faintly, her wings translucent as morning dew, her laughter as soft as chimes heard from afar. Once, she had danced through cities and forests alike. Now she rarely left the branches of the Oak.The last Earth Sprite—small, round, and clad in moss—lived among its roots. His hands were always warm, always covered in soil. He remembered the feeling of mountains before they had been given names by humans.They were the last of their kind.Not because the others had fallen in battle—but because the World no longer remembered them.The child who still believed in magic lay far away in her bed. She had fallen into this sleep while still very young—her breath calm, her face peaceful, her hands curled as though holding an unseen dream.Something had happened.No one could agree on what it was. Some blamed illness. Some a curse. Some did not think about it at all.It was the Lord of Dreams.He saw what others could not. He had seen her potential. And so he carried the child into his realm, where time does not gnaw and sleep does not decay. He wrapped her in dreams—vast and luminous—dreams of cities that floated, of Worlds healed, of people walking hand in hand without fear.“She must not wake,” he said softly. “Not yet. If she does, magic will die.”And so the child slept.And the heart of the Oak kept beating.The World beyond the realm of dreams, beyond the Forest of the Great Golden Oak, changed. Generations came and went. Empires argued whether truth lay in numbers or in gods. But although the World around her kpet changing—the child did not. The girls body aged. But it aged so slowly, that many generations of her ancestors still only knew her as a child.Magic became a word used only by children—and then not even by them.Now the Elf and the Earth Sprite left the Oak by night. They stepped into dreams.They visited the child’s dreams as companions.Sometimes she was a Queen, standing before a divided World. Sometimes a wanderer, healing wounds with her hands. Sometimes she was simply herself—laughing, kind, asking questions that made even the stars pause.“She dreams so greatly,” the Earth Sprite once whispered, watching her build a bridge between two warring lands with nothing but words.“She always has,” the Elf answered quietly.They guarded her dreams for ages.And longer still.But the Oak grew weaker. Each year its heart beat more slowly. The Elf’s glow faded. The Earth Sprite’s steps grew heavy. Magic did not return. The World had become too full of certainty to leave space for wonder.One day, the Wind turned.\r\nStories_of_the_Wind_Collectibles.odt\r\nIt carried distress. Despair. The World trembled—not with earthquakes, but with a fracture so sharp it cut into the unseen roots of reality itself.The Elf rested her forehead against the bark of the Oak. “It is time,” she whispered.The Earth Sprite trembled. “Should we truly do this?” he asked. “If we wake her... no magic will remain. Not for us. Not for anyone.”The Elf knelt beside him. “What is magic for,” she asked gently, “if not to give the World hope? To fill it with life?”She placed her hand upon the Oak.“If this is our fate,” she continued, her voice steady despite her fear, “then we must accept it.”They listened—not with their ears, but with their hearts.And the Oak answered.They stepped into the deepest dream.The Lord of Dreams saw them coming. Sorrow already lay in his eyes. “You know what this will cost.” he said.“We do.” said the elf.“And you will not ask to be remembered.” he added.The Earth Sprite shook his head. “Only that she wakes.”They touched the child’s dream.And let go.Magic unraveled—not violently, but gently. Like a final song sung into the bones of the World. The heart of the Oak flared once, brilliant and golden, and sent its last pulse through every root and every stone.A single golden tear of resin fell—its farewell, given in love and sorrow.The child stirred.She awoke.The girl awoke into a changed World. She lived. She walked among humankind. She spoke words that unitedfactions who had thought themselves enemies. She reminded science of wonder, and faith of kindness. She reminded all who clung too tightly to ideals, knowledge, laws, or belief that there was room enough within a single mind to hold both—to accept new understanding, and still leave space for the small, uncertain, and wondrous things that remained.She did not call it magic.She called it peace.She taught people to feel it, first inside them, within themselves. And then, to carry it into their life.When she died—old, content, and after a life fulfilled—the World felt quieter.And beneath the Great Oak lay two small, still forms.A tulip grew where the Elf rested.A forget-me-not where the Earth Sprite slept.\r\nStories_of_the_Wind_Collectibles.odt\r\nIn a later age, people would say that magic had vanished on that day. They would say no one could have prevented it.No one would know of two small pairs of hands upon ancient bark, and of a choice made without mortal witness.Only the Wind knows the truth.");
        myTexts.Add("When people speak of the great ages of Dashuri, they speak of kings and generals, of priests and mages, of battles that shifted borders, and of miracles that opened gates between Worlds.Rarely do they speak of Druids and Bards.And when they do, it is often with a smile—kind, but condescending. Storytellers. Forest folk. Dreamers.The Wind knows better.When the World first began to heal—when magic still flowed freely through roots and rivers—it was not the powerful who held everything together.It was the quiet ones.The Druids did not rule. They listened. They learned the rhythm of the seasons, the language of the soil, the sorrow of trees felled too soon, and the suffering of rivers forced into beds of stone. They felt when the balance shifted—not with instruments, but deep within their souls. This gave them more foresight, and more influence over fate, than any ruler.The Bards did not command. They remembered. They wove history into melodies, warnings into rhyme, hope into lullabies sung to children who did not yet know how fragile the World truly was. Their songs were never written down—for what is written can be burned. Songs carried on breath are both fleeting and unbreakable. And their power is stronger than any command.When the first cracks appeared—at first small, barely visible—it was the Druids who felt the pain. Magic grew thin. The roots became restless. The spirits withdrew. The Druids warned the councils of the realms. They spoke to kings and priests alike.“Something is wrong,” they said. “The World is forgetting itself.”\r\nStories_of_the_Wind_Collectibles.odt\r\nThey were thanked.Then ignored.It was the Bards who noticed next. The songs began to lose their sound. Old melodies lost their power. Children forgot verses their grandparents swore they had once sung perfectly.So the Bards changed their songs.Not openly. They hid truth within humor, prophecy within love songs, memory within tavern tunes that people hummed without ever knowing why they felt comforted and at ease.When conflict finally broke out—when magic and faith were set against one another like enemies—the Druids and the Bards chose no “side.”They simply chose the people.Druids guided refugees through forests that shifted to protect them. They created paths where none existed and closed ways to those who would bring harm. Bards carried messages across war-torn lands, disguised as fools and actors. They spread hope where despair might have ended all resistance, and planted doubt where blind fanaticism threatened to harden the hearts.They never claimed glory.They never stayed long enough for their faces or names to be remembered.But their message endured.When the gate between Worlds finally opened—at that impossible place in the heart of the desert—it was said that priests, mages, and warriors all had equally shown courage and made sacrifices to bring about that miracle.That was true.But it was incomplete.For long before that moment, the Druids had prepared the land, slowly healing the places where magic could still take root. And the Bards had kept the idea of unity alive long enough that anyone still believed it could exist at all.Without them, the gate would have opened into nothing.Many generations later, when the World began to forget once more, it happened quietly. Stories were no longer told. Songs were replaced by noise. Forests shrank. Magic withdrew like a wounded creature.Again, the Druids and the Bards acted.This time, they did not fight the forgetting. They adapted.The Druids withdrew to the last places where belief still lingered—into the deep forests of Shpresa, beneath the Great Golden Oak. They became caretakers rather than guides, keeping the heart of the World beating a little longer.The Bards changed their craft once more. They sang fewer obvious songs of magic. Instead, they sang of friendship, of courage, of a love that endured across time and boundaries. They trusted that magic would recognize itself again when the moment came.And it did.When the last Elf and the last Earth Sprite came to rest, there was no despair.\r\nStories_of_the_Wind_Collectibles.odt\r\nThere was silence.A silence gently guarded by Druids who kept watch, and by Bards who no longer sang aloud—but instead whispered their thoughts to the Wind.Maybe they could feel, that one day in the distant future, in a World devoid of magic, when the final tear of the Oak gave birth to new hope, it would be decided whether magic—and with it, life—would return to the World.And a new cycle would begin.But that would happen much, much later.Some said magic had faded because humankind forgot. They said no one had tried to stop it.They were wrong.The World did not endure because of heroes whose deeds were praised. It endured because of heroes who chose not to be known.And though their names are gone, the echo of their work still hums in roots and in rhyme—in those momentswhen people feel hope and trust without knowing why.People no longer remember the songs, the stories, the countless small and great acts of that time. Not every hand that healed. Not every truth that was revealed and yet remained hidden.Only the Wind knows the truth.");
        myTexts.Add("Dritoria was a land where dawn lingered.Each morning, the sunlight seemed reluctant to rise fully over hills and towers, as though even the day wished to reflect a little longer before completely arriving in the land. Scholars said it was the sacred radiance of Dritan, the God of Light. Farmers said it was because the land was kind. Priests said both were true.And perhaps they were right.In a small village, not far from the great academies, lived a man named Seren. He was not important. He wore no fine garments, held no titles, and wrote no books that would outlive him. His work was that of a lamplighter and field hand—by night he tended the oil lamps along the roads, and by day he helped the farmers wrest grain from the stubborn soil.But Seren possessed a gift.He listened.When Seren was young, he had studied for a time at one of the smaller schools. He learned letters, numbers, philosophy, and theology. He learned how scholars debated truth and how priests argued doctrine.And he learned something else.Everyone was certain they were right.\r\nStories_of_the_Wind_Collectibles.odt\r\nSo Seren left. Not in anger. Not in disappointment. Simply because he felt that wisdom, if it truly belonged tothe light, should not blind.He returned to the fields.Each morning, Seren rose before dawn and stood in stillness as the first light spread across the land. He spoke no prayers aloud. He asked for no favors. He simply acknowledged the light—and then went to work.People came to him, though they did not quite know why.A young priest once sat beside him, troubled by doubt. Seren did not argue over sacred texts. He only asked, “Does your faith make you kinder?”A scholar once mocked the idea of magic. Seren asked, “Does something cease to be beautiful simply because we understand how it works?”To a farmer mourning a failed harvest, Seren said, “The land remembers care. Try again.”They left feeling lighter.They never called him wise—not in words. And yet each one of his visitors left a little wiser than they had been when they approached him.As the years passed, Dritoria changed. Faith grew rigid. Knowledge became sharp-edged. Light became something to possess, rather than to share. Scholars dismissed what could not be measured. Priests grew wary of what did not fit their doctrine.The quiet magic that had once been present began to fade.Seren felt it first—not as a loss, but as an absence.Dawn still came.But it no longer lingered.One winter morning, Seren found a lamp by the roadside that would not stay lit. No matter how carefully he trimmed the wick, no matter how pure the oil, the flame flickered and died.He tried again. And again.At last, he cupped the lamp in his hands and whispered—not a prayer, not a spell, but a memory.Of warmth.Of shared meals.Of questions asked without fear.The flame held.The Wind brushed gently along the road and lingered beside him, as though watching.As the great conflict between faith and magic drew near, Seren chose no side.Instead, he walked.He carried light from village to village—sometimes as a true flame, sometimes as a conversation that softened anger, sometimes as a question that unsettled certainty just enough for compassion to enter.No one noticed.But wherever he went, fewer torches were raised in hatred.\r\nStories_of_the_Wind_Collectibles.odt\r\nOne evening, Seren did not return home.At dawn, he was found sitting at a crossroads, his back resting against a boundary stone. A lamp stood besidehim, still burning. His face was peaceful.A priest said he had died in prayer.A scholar said his heart had simply failed.A farmer said the light had called him home.They buried him in silence.The lamp burned for a time upon his grave—until one day, it was simply gone.Years later, scholars wrote that Dritoria had lost magic because people had outgrown superstition. Priests said faith alone had replaced it. Farmers said the land had changed—though they did not know whether for better or worse.And yet, sometimes, at dawn—when the light lingers just one breath longer than it should—some travelers swear they have seen a single burning lamp at an empty crossroads.No oil.No wick.Only light.The truth is only known to the Wind.");

        myTexts.Add("Agim was never a land of kings.It was a land of Houses.Warrior families, their names carved into steel and stone. Their stories not sung, but forged. Strength, discipline, mastery—these were the virtues that shaped Agim. Power was not seized through bloodshed alone, but earned through endurance, integrity, and wisdom.Among these Houses, none was more renowned than House Kareth.They were not conquerors.They were guardians.House Kareth was sworn to protect a vault buried deep beneath their ancestral stronghold. No map marked its entrance. No inscription named its contents. Each generation was taught only what was necessary:Guard it. Never open it. Speak of it to no one.The reason had long been lost, reduced to a handful of words passed down through time.One Lord of the House was Ardan Kareth. He was everything Agim admired: a master of arms, a peerless tactician, a man whose pure will could bend iron and whose discipline and courage never faltered. He believed in mastery above all things—the mastery of body, mind, and fear. Magic, he believed, was merely a crutch.\r\nStories_of_the_Wind_Collectibles.odt\r\nHe said so openly.Agim applauded.When the World began to fracture—when rumors spread of priests and mages at odds, of magic fading and surging back in unstable waves—envoys came to Ardan. They spoke of artifacts. Of power. Of a chance to shape the fate of the World.Ardan refused them all.“Agim does not rule through miracles,” he said. “We endure through skill.”But the vault beneath his feet stirred.For the first time in generations, a member of his House dreamed of fire. It was the fire of a forge—a fire that had guided many of his ancestors, leading them through visions in times of need. In his dream, the fire revealed the path downward, to the place no one had entered for centuries.He descended into the vault alone.What he found was no weapon.It was an oath.A blade, yes—plain at first glance. No jewels. No glow. Only fine runes, worn smooth by time. When he touched it, truth flooded him.House Kareth had once been entrusted with a choice no one else dared to make.The blade was a focus—not of power, of destruction.... but of decision.Whoever wielded it could tip the balance. If the World stood on the brink of dividing into one realm with magic and one without, this artifact could sever them forever—or open a path to make them whole again.To wield it required mastery of the self.Ardan stepped back.He told no one.Agim gathered its strength. Armies formed. Pressure mounted. Other houses whispered that Kareth was hiding something—that they feared the coming age, and the changes stirring across the other continents.Something was happening in the World.Ardan stood before a mirror and saw something new in his reflection:Doubt.He had mastered everything—everything but the uncertainty of whether this one choice would be the right one.When the final call came—when Agim demanded he reveal the secret his house guarded, that he use it for the sake of the land—Ardan acted.He did not summon an army.Instead, he descended into the vault.\r\nStories_of_the_Wind_Collectibles.odt\r\n“It is not for me to decide the fate of the World,” he said softly. “It would be easy to break the blade, and deny this choice to anyone forever. It would be easy to use it for my own ends. Or to hand it to one side or the other—to mages or to priests. It would be easy to turn away from this burden.”He took the blade and knelt.“But I will uphold the oath.”He drove the blade into the stone, renewing the vow. He resolved to guard and strengthen the vault so that none could enter—and to find another way to face the unrest in the land.“One day...” he said to himself. “One day, someone will come who can bear this choice. If not I, then one of my descendants will meet them. When my House swears an oath, it is kept. And to honor the weight of this vow, it shall be the last oath my family ever swears.”With newfound certainty and strength, Ardan walked through the vaults with steady resolve.“If true mastery means choosing wisely, then let this be Agim’s greatest victory.”No one would ever know that the proud warrior had felt uncertainty in that one single moment of his life. Noone would ever know the weight of the choice made that day. And no one, save the head of House Kareth himself, would ever know what lay hidden beneath the fortress of that proud warrior family.Only the Wind knows the truth.");
        myTexts.Add("A Tale of Two SistersTwo sisters were born beneath the same sky, at the same hour, as the Wind stirred the curtains of the birthing room and set the chimes above the door ringing.The midwives said the air itself seemed to hold its breath as the girls cried out together for the first time, as though the World had briefly forgotten which voice belonged to whom.They were named Aerin and Vaela.From the beginning, they resembled each other only in the things that mattered the least. Aerin laughed easily, restless and drawn to open fields and high places where the Wind ran free. Vaela was quieter, thoughtful, content to sit by the hearth or beneath old trees, listening to stories and watching the sky change its colors.And yet, though their paths diverged early, something unseen bound them together—something no scholar could measure and no priest or mage could have fully explained.As children, they often spoke at the same time, finishing each other’s sentences without noticing. When apart, they would suddenly pause, struck by a feeling not entirely their own—a warmth in the chest, a sharp flicker of worry, a joy without apparent cause.They learned, without being told, that these moments belonged to the other.As the years passed, life carried them in different directions. Aerin left home, following old roads, chasing horizons and half-forgotten songs. Vaela remained, tending the land and its people, becoming a quiet pillar ofher village.\r\nStories_of_the_Wind_Collectibles.odt\r\nThey wrote when they could, but words were a poor exchange compared to the knowing that existed betweenthem. Their bond bridged every distance—like the Wind.Aerin swore that whenever she laughed, the breeze seemed to answer, as though someone far away laughed with her. When Vaela woke at night, she sometimes felt a smile upon her lips without reason—and knew thenthat her sister was safe and well.When Aerin fell ill in a distant land, Vaela awoke with a deep pain in her bones, feeling a sickness without a name. She sat by the Window for days, listening to the Wind as though it might carry answers. And when Aerin recovered—laughing weakly at her own stubborn survival—Vaela felt the pain leave her before any letter arrived.But time, patient yet unyielding, claims what is its due.It was on a quiet morning, unmarked by a storm or some other grand sign from above, that death came for Aerin. There was no great struggle, no final cry—only a breath released, unfollowed by one drawn afterwards.Far away, Vaela staggered as though struck. Her heart faltered, and she fell to her knees, certain that something irreplaceable had been taken from the World.The village mourned with her. They spoke of loss, of endings, of the finality of death.Vaela listened.But she did not quite believe them.For in the days that followed, she began to notice small things. The Wind lingered longer than before. It wrapped around her shoulders on cold nights. It rustled the pages of her books at just the right moment.And sometimes—only sometimes—she heard laughter in the air. Soft. Familiar. Less a sound than the echo of a memory.In dreams, Aerin walked beside her—no longer bound by roads or by years left to live. She spoke not in words, but in feeling:I am here. I have not left you.Vaela lived on, growing older as all mortals must. Her hair turned silver, her hands grew lined—but she was never alone. On hills, at open Windows, the Wind always found her.And when her own final hour came, there was no fear in her eyes.Those who stood beside her said the room filled with a gentle breeze—warm and bright, though the day outside was still. Vaela smiled, as though greeting someone long awaited.They were never separated.Not by distance.Not by death.No one would ever truly know how deep the bond of their sisterly love had been.The truth was ony known by the Wind.");
        myTexts.Add("Long before the borders of Dashuri were drawn upon parchment, before roads cut through the wild and hills and rivers were given names, there was a Forest that belonged only to itself.The people who later lived at its edge called it the Old Green, though no one could say how old it truly was. The trees there grew taller than any tower, their roots winding through stone and earth like slow-moving serpents. The Wind moved differently beneath their crowns—quieter, gentler—and carried whispers more often than storms.In this Forest there once lived a young doe, scarcely more than a year old. She was slender and cautious, her coat still soft with youth. She had learned the paths, the streams, and the safe clearings from her mother, whohad taught her one lesson above all others:Run when the Forest tells you to run.One winter morning, when frost still clung to the moss and the World held its breath, the doe wandered farther than she ever had before. Hunger had driven her—the winter was harsh, and food was scarce. She followed the scent of bark and lichen into a part of the Forest where the trees stood closer together and the light thinned into pale threads. Snow rested upon the branches and drifted down in quiet places.\r\nStories_of_the_Wind_Collectibles.odt\r\nThen the Forest cried out.Not in sound—but in a feeling that seized the animal.A sudden tension filled the air. The birds fell silent. The Wind stilled. The doe froze—but it was too late.High above, in the brittle crowns, old wood gave way. A great branch, heavy with ice and marked by decadesof decay, broke loose with a thunderous crack. It crashed down, pinning the doe’s hind leg and pressing her into a hollow of frozen mud and roots.She cried out—a sound that tore the silence like a snapping branch.The weight held her fast. Each time she struggled, the rough bark bit deeper into her flesh, and the frozen ground gripped her like stone. Blood darkened the snow beneath her.Hours passed.And more hours.The cold began to slow her heart.As her strength faded, she noticed movement nearby—low to the ground, swift and deliberate.A vixen emerged between the roots of an ancient Oak. She was older, her muzzle dusted with grey, one eye clouded by an old wound. Foxes were known for cunning, not for courage—and certainly not for approaching suffering creatures trapped beneath the weight of the forest. For where a wounded animal lay, the hunters of the woods were rarely far.But this vixen did not flee.She circled the doe slowly, studying the fallen trunk, her ears twitching at every distant sound. The doe expected teeth—painful, merciful, final.Instead, the vixen sat.She studied the trunk and the hollow for a long time, tilting her head from side to side, as though she listenednot only to the creaking wood, but to the Forest itself.Then she acted.With careful precision, the vixen began to dig away the frozen earth beneath the doe’s leg—where the body had already given some warmth to the ground. Her claws bled as the hard soil resisted her, but she did not stop. She bit through roots and dragged earth aside, widening the space beneath the heavy branch.It took hours.The doe lost consciousness once—perhaps twice.When she woke, the crushing weight had eased. The vixen had dug deep enough that the doe’s leg could slip free into the hollow she had made.The doe collapsed, her leg wounded—but free.The vixen pressed her nose briefly against the doe’s flank—a silent gesture, a farewell and a promise—and vanished into the undergrowth.The doe survived.The wound healed slowly, leaving a scar and a slight limp that never fully faded. She carried it for the rest of her life—a quiet memory etched into flesh.\r\nStories_of_the_Wind_Collectibles.odt\r\nYears passed.The Old Green changed, as forests do. Trees fell and grew anew. Streams shifted their course. The doe grew older, wiser, and in time became a mother. She raised her fawns deep within the Forest and taught them to listen to the Wind.One spring, when the World had turned green again, the doe sensed danger long before she saw it.The wolves had returned.A pack—young, hungry, desperate.She smelled them in the air, and panic rose within her. Her fawns scattered, as she had taught them—but not all were swift enough. The wolves encircled them.The doe ran, drawing them away, her injured leg burning with every step. She knew she could not outrun them forever.Just as her strength began to fail, the Forest shifted.Movement came from the shadows—swift, coordinated, purposeful.Foxes.Not one, but many.A whole pack, their red coats flashing between roots and ferns. They darted at the wolves, snapping at their heels, vanishing and reappearing elsewhere, confusing and dividing the pack. The Forest itself seemed to aid them—branches caught in the hunters’ fur, uneven ground turned against them.At their head stood an old vixen with a clouded eye.The wolves, disoriented and frustrated, finally withdrew—not driven off by strength, but by persistence and cunning.The doe stood trembling, her breath unsteady.The vixen approached her slowly.For a long moment, they regarded one another—two lives bound by a past neither could speak of.Then the vixen turned and led her pack back into the depths of the Green.The fawns emerged unharmed from the undergrowth.Seasons passed.Years later, long after the doe had died and her bones had returned to the earth, a young she-wolf kept watch over her litter at the forest’s edge. One of the pups wandered too far and tumbled toward a hidden ravine.A fox—old, grey, and weary—darted forward, seized the pup by the scruff, and pulled it to safety before vanishing again, too swift for the wolf to react.The wolf never knew why the fox had done this.She only knew that the Forest had protected her child.And so the grace of the Old Green, carried through the foxes, was passed on to all creatures equally—a deep memory, a quiet instinct woven into the living fabric of the forest.The people who lived nearby sometimes said the Forest had a soul.\r\nStories_of_the_Wind_Collectibles.odt\r\nThey were not wrong.They also said it brought good fortune to glimpse a fox. Some even whispered that a particularly kind, devoted, and wise vixen served the Old Green as its spirit.And when the Wind moves softly through the trees, carrying the scent of moss and earth, it whispers of kindness given without expectation—and carried onward through generations.No human ever truly knew what unfolded within the Old Green.Only the Wind knows the truth.");
        myTexts.Add("The three friends Yuki, Abed, and Lal lived in the small yet idyllic village of Miko, at the border of the land of Okoku on the continent of Dritoria. Their home lay exactly between the forest and the desert.Aside from their families, there were a few other people in the village whom they had grown especially fond of: the kind merchant Laros, who always had a sweet ready when the children went shopping for their parents; the old fisherman Taman, who had returned to his homeland after a long and eventful life at sea, and who always had exciting and instructive stories to tell when the children sat with him by the water...But most of all, they loved Sofu.Everyone in the village showed him great respect and told the children that it had been he who made Miko into such a flourishing place—one that, despite its popularity, had never lost its quiet charm and peace. Sofu loved all children, but he had a special place for Yuki, Abed, and Lal in his heart.He would sit with them as they played and tell them stories of distant lands filled with magic. He especially loved to tell them about Shpresa, and about the wondrous songs of the Bards and spells of the Druids, whose eldest were said to guard the secrets of the world. Many legends surrounded their true power. It was said that anyone who managed to earn a Bard’s or a Druid’s trust could only be pure of heart—for their wise gaze looked directly into the soul.One of the stories they heard was directly connected to the village of Miko.\r\nStories_of_the_Wind_Collectibles.odt\r\nThe mysterious storm that swept through the desert every thirteen years, making the caravan road there impassable for a time, would soon return and render the paths unusable once more, making any journey into the desert very dangerous. But of course, only for those who did not know what to watch for in order to protect themselves...The three friends began to discuss it among themselves. They had heard that the storm had once been caused by an evil mage, who had been banished forever into his tower by the brave priests of Dritan. And that this tower appeared only during the time of the storm...Perhaps they could find the mage’s tower—and become great heroes, like those in the old stories and legends.Surely the storm would begin slowly, and for at least a few days it could not yet be too dangerous to search for it...“I want to be a great hero!” Yuki cried, raising her wooden sword heroically.“But Mama said we mustn’t go so far into the desert to play anymore. It’s dangerous,” Abed warned.“Are you sure you want to look for the tower?” Lal asked.“Yes,” Yuki answered—without hesitation and without fear.“Well then,” said Lal, “how about a compromise? We can keep watch near my home, and as soon as we see the storm, we run to safety. We can make a small expedition, use the lookout—and if things get dangerous, we just run back to my house. Would that be alright?”Abed nodded shyly, but he seemed content.“Hey! Come on! A little more excitement!” Yuki called out. “This will be our greatest adventure!”Laughing, she pulled her friends close and held her fist out in the middle.“As long as we are there for each other, nothing bad can ever happen to us.”She beamed at her friends.“Friends forever!”Lal laughed and placed his hand on Yuki’s fist.Abed smiled and no longer seemed as worried.“Friends forever,” he said, placing his right hand on Lal’s.Just as they had climbed onto the highest rock they knew in that area, and still could see nothing in the sky, they were about to give up—when Abed suddenly called out:“Look—what is that strange glow?”And indeed: far in the distance, just barely visible through the spyglass, within a shimmering light, the dark outline of a tower could be seen. Lal confirmed that he and Father Vaelin had often been in that area, but had never seen the tower before.Excited, the three looked at one another.“If we run there, we won’t be back until morning. Father Vaelin will be angry,” Lal considered.Yuki, on the other hand, was bursting with excitement.\r\nStories_of_the_Wind_Collectibles.odt\r\n“Then what are we waiting for? Do you think the great heroes succeeded in their adventures because they were always home in time for supper?”And so it was decided.The three friends could not pass up this incredible discovery. They ran toward the tower.When they arrived at the tower, a cold shiver ran down their spines.“Is the storm already coming?” Abed asked anxiously.“No,” Lal answered, looking around. “The sky is clear.”“It must be the evil magic of the sorcerer,” Yuki said firmly.There was a strange force—at once like a pull that tugged at them, and like an invisible wall they had to pushforward with effort in order to move ahead.“It is said in some stories that the priests of Dritan imprisoned the evil mage. If it truly is him, then we should not be in any danger,” said Lal.The heavy wooden door at first seemed like an insurmountable obstacle, until Lal peered through the narrow gap and realized that it was not locked.They went inside.As they stepped in, their hearts stopped in fright and seemed to forget to beat.There he stood:the master mage.Within a circle of runes that glowed with magic.“Something is wrong,” Abed whispered. “He doesn’t look as though he is imprisoned!”Startled, he ran to the side to hide behind a crate. Lal had already taken cover behind a pillar. Only Yuki remained where she stood—stubborn despite her fear—and placed her hand on her wooden sword.Abed stumbled and fell against a shelf, which toppled over. A jug shattered, and its contents spilled across the markings of the circle.The mage, apparently not noticing the children, continued casting frantically and cried out loudly:“KAZAAHM! KAZAAHM!”Yuki heard Lal shout something and he started running, but she could not make out what he said. Still, she grabbed Abed’s hand when he was in reach, and ran with him.Behind them, the tower collapsed.Yuki awoke in her bed.She had changed.Parts of her body bore strange, animal-like features. Her ears and eyes were like that of a cat. And she had a foxes tail.She learned that the storm had passed, and that the remains of a dead child had been found in the desert.\r\nStories_of_the_Wind_Collectibles.odt\r\nFor many long years, she lived with the guilt of having caused the deaths of her two friends—even though, because of the trauma of the event, she no longer remembered what had happened.And yet, she could never rid herself of the feeling that they were not entirely lost.After thirteen years, she entered the tower again.She remembered that they had interrupted the mage during a ritual—and that something had gone wrong—and she discovered the mage’s journal.Time seemed to pass differently within the tower.What he had written made her think—and stirred her curiosity.After long journeys that took Yuki across all three continents, and after many conversations in which Sofu gave her wise counsel, she returned home.She had to uncover the secret of the tower at last—and save her friends.There had to be a way.In her heart, she could feel that they were not yet lost.On her travels, she learned of the division of the world:that the clergy was trying to separate the magical part of the world forever—while the mages sought to prevent this.One of the most fanatical servants of Dritan pursued her.When Yuki freed the master mage in the tower from his prison—which she and her friends had once created themselves through their own mishap—in order to learn what had happened to her friends, a struggle broke out between the mage and the priest who now entered through the door. He had followed her...A gift from a wise old warrior of Agim—a powerful artifact—was capable of deciding the fate of the world.He had entrusted it to her, knowing that she was in the process of uncovering the truth behind the history of the continents.And because she had been born human, yet was also marked by magic, he placed the artifact in her hands—and with it, the decision over the fate of the world.But far more important:Yuki realized that she could save her friends.Yuki did not want to allow magic to vanish from the world—and so she helped the mage.Both opponents—the mage and the priest—struck each other down.And the priest also tried to kill Yuki.But Sofu threw himself between them.Realizing that Sofu—who had suddenly appeared and sacrificed himself to save her—was in truth Lal, Yuki broke down in tears.Before he closed his eyes forever, he whispered:\r\nStories_of_the_Wind_Collectibles.odt\r\n“It was so hard... I wanted so much to tell you... to warn you. But then all the good would never have come to pass... I have achieved so much for our village. I was able to help so many people... I could not do that to them. I could not undo this—I could not take all of that away from them.”Tears filled his aged eyes.“I know you can save Abed. None of us has to suffer anything terrible. I know you feel it too. Deep inside. We are there for each other.”He placed his hand on hers.“Friends...” His eyes closed.Before he could finish the sentence, his strength left him.He released it with his final breath.“Friends forever...” Yuki whispered, barely audible, her voice choked with emotion, and placed her hands upon his chest.If Lal had been cast back through time by the magical accident back then, then it had to be possible to reach Abed... didn’t it? The mage had been trapped in the magic Rift. Being able to share his knowledge wit Lal. But Lal got through to the other side. And Abed and her had been left behind.And now, the magic was awakening again. But she could feel in her bones, that this was the last time the Tower would appear in the storm. Her last chance.Not knowing whether it might cost her life, and whether she should instead flee the tower, Yuki approached the magical ritual circle once more—the place where everything had begun.In a vision, a kind of magical window, she watched as the events of her past unfolded one final time:how the mage and Lal were caught in the spell of time and swept away—and as Abed ran outward, toward the young Yuki of that time, to take her hand—the grown Yuki, following an impulse, reached out her own hand.Partly guided by an instinct that had lived within her since that day, partly by the longing for the dear friend she had lost.She grasped the young Abed, pulled him toward her—and once more, a last time—Yuki and Abed ran out from the ruins of the tower, whose last standing remains now collapsed behind them for good.When they arrived in Miko, Abed slept for several days.Yuki took him in as her own child.Like her back then, he could remember nothing after he awoke—and he lived a long and happy life in his Home village. Never knowing who his mother really was.Three children from the small village of Miko had forged a bond of friendship that was stronger than space and time.They did not abandon one another.Each of the children grew up and lived a long and fulfilled life.\r\nStories_of_the_Wind_Collectibles.odt\r\nTwo of them carried the secret to their graves—that they had lived this life in different times.And yet, their lives had touched one another.Three children.Three generations.No human would ever learn of it.And no human would ever truly know how magic had returned to the world.For only the wind knows the truth.");

        myTexts.Add("Long ago, in a World called Dashuri, the cycle of life had reached its end once more.As it had many times before, the age of magic came to a close.A turning point had come.For with the end of magic, life itself in the World would also fade. Would this be the final ending? Would the World truly die after this cycle—leaving only spirits and their memories behind?Filled with love, joy, and a quiet sorrow, the Great Golden Oak shed its final tear—along with the last of its life’s strength.The last golgen drop of magic.The final hope for the World, should it ever be brought back to life.Time passed.A long, long time.Until, without magic, the World slowly withered and died. No life remained within it.\r\nStories_of_the_Wind_Collectibles.odt\r\nSave for a single place.A tiny barely noticable spot, where life could be born one last time.From the tear that had once been shed, a long time ago, a small and innocent tree creature took shape.It did not know who it was, nor why it had come into the World. But within its heart, it felt love, joy, and sorrow—and curiosity. It sensed that, if it searched long enough, it would one day understand its origin and its purpose.So it began its journey.Along the way, it encountered small lights.They were the spirits of animals. Some of them even still had their complete Soul.The smallest were firefly spirits, who, after death, had become wandering lights. They and other animal spirits, too—those not yet corrupted like some of the darker spirits that turned into evil Ghosts—guided it safely on its path.They granted it fragments of the abilities they had once possessed in life.The first kind Soul it met belonged to a Hare.From that moment on, the little creature was filled with energy and boundless agility, able to leap farther thanever before.But the darker spirits, the Ghosts—it had to avoid.They were filled with sorrow, or anger, or fear. The loss of magic, the absence of life, had twisted them. Theyharmed the little creature, perhaps out of envy—for it seemed to be the only thing in the World still truly alive.The little creature did not wish to hurt them.Yet when it cast its light upon them, they became peaceful for a short while.It remembered that.The little creature wondered what else it might discover in the World—and whether it would ever learn how and why it had come to be.");
        myTexts.Add("The little creature overcame many dangers.It was a vast and shadowed World—but it was also beautiful, in its own quiet, ghostly way. The longer the little creature wandered, the more it felt as though it knew this World.And it sensed that the World, too, was remembering something.Each time it touched the ground, each time it leapt across a stone, each time it brought even the smallest lightinto the darkness—it felt as though a memory stirred.“If I simply keep going,” the little creature thought, “then surely I will remember as well... remember who I am.”With a pure and hopeful heart, it ventured even into the darkest places.At the edge of the forest, the thorned hedges grew ever denser. Yet it discovered the entrance to a cave. Bravely, it stepped inside, curious where the tunnels might lead.It had already met the soul of a hare—and earlier, the spirit of a bird. How wondrous it had been to glide across the ground, weightless, drifting through the air.But all that drifting meant nothing in this complete darkness.\r\nStories_of_the_Wind_Collectibles.odt\r\nThen—two eyes flashed in the black.Fearless, the small being approached.The soul of a cat purred softly and gifted it her eyes and ears. The little creature felt that the cat wished it a safe journey.Suddenly—everything was bright.For the first time in its life, the little creature blinked.It slowly opened its eyes again—for a sound echoed through the cave. A steady dripping. Had that been therebefore?The World was becoming richer.Overwhelmed by these new, vivid senses, it moved carefully through the tunnels. It had to be cautious—sharp edges could cut it, unseen drops could swallow it whole. There were no dark spirits here, but the earth itself held its own dangers.And yet—even here—it found the stories of the Wind.All along its journey, it delighted in how much the Wind could tell of the World. Grateful to have a listener who could truly understand, the Wind whispered countless tales to it—of all lands, of all times.It was as though the little creature was the World itself, able to witness and experience these stories as they unfolded.How sad, that none but the Wind remembered them—no one truly knew what had once come to pass.For the World had been a treasure beyond measure, filled with stories— when it was alive.");
        myTexts.Add("It was no longer cold.When the little creature reached the surface once more, it felt the heat of the sun burning upon it. After all thedarkness and cold, it welcomed the light with quiet joy. Even in the forest, it had never seen the sun so clearly—only scattered rays falling through the canopy.Here, the sky stood open above it.And yet, it sensed something else.Though the place was empty, it felt as though life had once existed here. It wondered how long ago that might have been. There were stones, and pillars, and strange tablets and figures marked with symbols.Runes, it thought—without knowing how it knew what they were called.Then new spirits appeared.\r\nStories_of_the_Wind_Collectibles.odt\r\nAn old skeleton lay before the little being. Part of its corrupted soul still seemed to remember what the creature had once been in life.“I must be careful,” the little being thought. “These are dark spirits again. Ghosts. I should avoid them.”Along its path, it encountered the soul of a curious and quirky little animal, who granted it the ability to curl into itself and roll forward with great force—almost unstoppable.It was a great help.With it, the little creature could break through certain walls, uncover hidden parts of the World, and evade dangers more easily. And while it rolled forward, almost nothing could harm it.But each time, it became terribly dizzy.“I should not do this too often,” it thought. “I will rest a little bit each time, after.”On ancient wall carvings, the little being saw three pillars, each marked with runes. Three of them glowed.“I should remember this,” it thought. “It might be important.”Before it rose a great mountain.Curious, and unafraid of falling, the little creature climbed. The Wind swept wildly from side to side, and it laughed with delight as it was carried through the air. When it chose the right moment, it could let the Wind and the wings of the bird’s soul carry it far.At last—it reached the summit.The little creature stepped onto a wide and open place.It recognized the three pillars.And it touched them.It felt a power within itself—something that awakened the runes. Magic flowed from the little creature into the symbols, and was multiplied a thousandfold.“This is so exciting!” it thought with joy—still unaware that something far greater was yet to come.Behind the pillars lay a pile of stone within a ritual circle.It began to move.The stones rose and formed a massive creature—a guardian made of stone.The ground trembled beneath its steps.The little creature dodged its heavy strikes and ran, instinctively seeking a way through. It avoided the monstrous guardian and reached a rune stone behind it. As soon as it touched the rune, the guardian fell silentonce more and returned to its slumber.The little creature paused.Should it continue?Then—it saw something new.Shimmering platforms appeared in the air, forming a path.As the runes shaped them into a staircase leading upward, the little being began to understand.\r\nStories_of_the_Wind_Collectibles.odt\r\nIt had been born of magic.“I am magic... like this stone being,” it thought. “That is why I could give the runes my power.”A new awareness stirred within it as it climbed higher.“I know now... what my purpose is,” it said softly, as it reached out and touched the final soul of its journey.It was the soul of a phoenix.");
        myTexts.Add("It had freed him—from his prison, being trapped in its death.For the Phoenix was no ordinary creature. It came back to life.“You are magic, just like me,” the little creature said, as it rose into the sky in the form of the Phoenix and flew back toward the forest—back to the Great Golden Oak.“Yes,” the Phoenix answered—carrying the little creature within himself, and at the same time allowing it to take his form. “We are both beings of magic. I have been waiting for you. If you wish, you can return magic to the World—and life. Do you wish to do that?”They flew together through the Winds, breathing in between the gusts, careful not to touch the dark spirits of the Forest below, whose presence already marked the nearing end of their journey.“Yes,” said the little creature.“You choose this... knowing the price?” the Phoenix asked again.\r\nStories_of_the_Wind_Collectibles.odt\r\n“Yes,” the little creature answered without hesitation. “I do. It is my purpose. I want to fulfill it, so that my life may have meaning.”“So it shall be,” said the Phoenix as they landed. “Then I shall grant you my power, as a farewell gift. When one life ends, another may begin.”The little creature was itself again. It parted from the Soul of the majestic creature that had returned it to its home.Yet it still felt the fire and strength of the Phoenix within its small green form.“When one life ends... another may begin,” it repeated softly.As it climbed the tree, the Wind moved gently around it, accompanying its ascent. It wished to ask the Wind something—but it was too joyful and distracted, leaping from leaf to branch. It had to remain careful, too, in case that Ghosts would appear.But none came.At last, it reached the silent heart of the Great Golden Oak.The little creature lay down and allowed the magic that had given it life to flow back into the heart of the tree.As it closed its eyes and drifted into an endless sleep, it whispered to the Wind:“Will you remember my story, too?”But before an answer could come, its small, gentle little heart beat for the final time.In that moment—when all life left the little creature—magic seeped like liquid, shimmering gold into the heart of the Great Oak.And the tree awakened.As its great golden heart began to beat once more with renewed strength—because the memory of magic hadbeen returned to the World, through a great sacrifice—the World itself began to awaken again.Slowly.Steadily.The corrupted spirits grew calm and found peace.New plants took root.And with the first returning magical beings, others followed—those ones who could once again shape and create new life.“This part of the Forest is beautiful,” said a small Forest spirit—one of the Earth Spirits who dwelled beneaththe soil.“Yes,” said its companion softly. “Let us call it the Old Green. I believe it will remain this beautifully green for a long time.”The little Earth Spirit and the small Elf set off together. They had much to do—but no need to hurry.After all, they had all the time in the World.\r\nStories_of_the_Wind_Collectibles.odt\r\nOne day, many beings would ask:“How did all of this begin?”“Where does magic come from?”“Will it last forever... or must it one day end again?”These questions—and many others—would, as always, remain unanswered.For the truth is known only to the Wind.");
    }

    public void OpenStoryPanel(int index)
    {
        myLoadMenu.ClickSoundPlayNoDeactivation();
        storyPanel.SetActive(true);
        collectionPanel.SetActive(false);
        storyImage.GetComponent<Image>().sprite = myButtons[index].GetComponent<Image>().sprite;
        caption.GetComponent<TMP_Text>().text = myCaptions[index].ToString();
        storyText.GetComponent<TMP_Text>().text = myTexts[index].ToString();
    }
}
